# AWS Best Practices

Apply AWS Well-Architected thinking to architecture and code.

Required considerations:

- least-privilege IAM
- encryption
- secret management
- idempotency
- retries/backoff
- timeouts
- DLQs/failure destinations where appropriate
- structured logging
- tracing/correlation
- metrics and alarms
- quotas and throttling
- concurrency and backpressure
- backups/restore where state matters
- cost implications
- Infrastructure as Code
- operational ownership

Prefer managed services where they reduce undifferentiated operational work.

For Lambda:

- keep handlers thin
- reuse clients/resources outside the handler where safe
- never rely on invocation-to-invocation in-memory state
- design for duplicate/retried events
- use explicit timeout and memory settings based on workload
- protect downstream systems using concurrency/backpressure where needed
